% SQLite. The second target, and the first evidence that the map is a map rather
% than one server's spellings written down twice.
%
% Read this file next to 03-functions.sqlt and 02-operators.sqlt: the same SEL,
% two documents, two very different renderings, no host code involved in the
% difference.
%
% The TRIM family is deliberately absent here and covered by sql/oracle instead:
% its template embeds a literal tab and newline, which a case file can hold but
% cannot show.

### name: sqlite.num.literals-are-text
--- note
The one that decides the shape of the dialect. SQLite has no exact decimal, and
2.50 as a bare literal is a REAL that prints as 2.5 -- so `2.50 $== 2.5` would be
TRUE here and FALSE in SEL. Quoted, SEL's exact characters survive and SQLite's
dynamic typing still reads them as a number: '2.50' + '2.50' is 5.0.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
2.50 + 2.50
--- expect
('2.50' + '2.50')
===
### name: sqlite.num.byte-comparison-sees-the-scale
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
"2.50" $== "2.5"
--- expect
(CAST('2.50' AS TEXT) = CAST('2.5' AS TEXT))
===
### name: sqlite.num.comparison-always-coerces
--- note
The consequence: two quoted numbers would compare as text, and SEL's == is
numeric. Both variants carry the same template, which is the data saying so.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
2.50 == 2.5
--- expect
(CAST('2.50' AS NUMERIC) = CAST('2.5' AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: sqlite.num.division-is-forced-real
--- note
SQLite's / between two integers is INTEGER division: 10 / 4 is 2 where SEL says
2.5. The * 1.0 makes it the same operation, inexactly, which decimal-float then
declares.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
10 / 4
--- expect
('10' * 1.0 / '4')
===
### name: sqlite.text.no-collation-needed
--- note
SQLite's default collation is BINARY, so textCollate is empty and 'A' = 'a' is
already 0. textCast is still there: without it two numeric operands compare as
numbers. Compare 02-operators.sqlt, where mariadb needs COLLATE utf8mb4_bin.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
"A" $== "a"
--- expect
(CAST('A' AS TEXT) = CAST('a' AS TEXT))
===
### name: sqlite.text.concat-is-an-operator
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
"a" & "b"
--- expect
('a' || 'b')
===
### name: sqlite.text.upper-needs-no-caveat
--- note
SQLite's upper() changes ASCII letters only, which is exactly what SEL
specifies -- so the ansi entry's unicode-case caveat is overridden away here,
where mysql-family has to keep it.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
UPPER("zażółć")
--- expect
upper('zażółć')
===
### name: sqlite.text.right-of-zero
--- note
substr(X, -N) alone is wrong for N = 0: -0 is 0, and substr(X, 0) behaves like
substr(X, 1), so RIGHT("a", 0) answered "a" where SEL says "". The explicit
length pins it. Found by the fuzz lane on sqlite's first run.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
RIGHT("a", 0)
--- expect
substr('a', -'0', '0')
===
### name: sqlite.text.code-point-in
--- note
Supported here and refused by mysql-family: SQLite's unicode() is a code point,
MariaDB's ORD is a byte sequence. The map earning its keep in the other
direction.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
CODE("é")
--- expect
unicode('é')
===
### name: sqlite.text.code-point-out
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
CHAR(233)
--- expect
char('233')
===
### name: sqlite.func.min-takes-one-argument
--- note
No arity-keyed template needed: min() accepts one argument where LEAST does not.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
MIN(1)
--- expect
min('1')
===
### name: sqlite.func.find-has-no-start-position
--- note
instr() takes no start position, so the entry narrows arity to [2,2] and the
three-argument call is refused. The map has documented `arity` as doing this
since M1 and nothing enforced it until sqlite was written -- see 13-review.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
FIND("a", "banana", 3)
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: sqlite.refuse.no-regexp
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
RMATCH('^a', "ab")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: sqlite.refuse.no-lpad
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
PADL("7", 3, "0")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: sqlite.strict.refuses-arithmetic
--- note
Every arithmetic entry carries decimal-float, so `strict` refuses the lot. That
is the option doing its job: a caller who needs SEL's contract rather than an
approximation of it gets a refusal instead of a number that is nearly right.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- options
{"strict": true}
--- source
2.50 + 2.50
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: sqlite.strict.allows-text
--- note
And the other half: the non-numeric side of this dialect is exact, so strict
passes it. If everything were caveated, `strict` would mean "refuse sqlite" and
the caveats would have stopped discriminating.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- options
{"strict": true}
--- source
UPPER("abc") $== "ABC"
--- expect
(CAST(upper('abc') AS TEXT) = CAST('ABC' AS TEXT))
===
### name: sqlite.bin.from-hex-is-refused
--- note
sqlite has no hex-to-blob function -- the MySQL family has UNHEX and PostgreSQL
has decode(x, 'hex') -- so the entry is a refusal here and a template there.
The refusal is the third arm of func.bin.from-hex; a partial refusal with only
its translating arms pinned is a dialect that can drift into translating.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
FROM_HEX("4869")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
